WEBVTT

1
00:00:14.271 --> 00:00:15.451
In Search of Dharma.

2
00:00:17.746 --> 00:00:20.091
2: The Genesis of Dharmas.

3
00:00:22.591 --> 00:00:25.091
Why does every society grow a dharma,

4
00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:27.771
and why did so many of the greatest ones
appear,

5
00:00:27.771 --> 00:00:30.731
in different worlds, at almost the same
moment?

6
00:00:31.811 --> 00:00:36.051
It starts with a problem that arrives the
day your village gets too big to know.

7
00:00:38.031 --> 00:00:41.811
Picture two human worlds, separated by ten
thousand years.

8
00:00:43.551 --> 00:00:46.351
In the first, a few dozen people move
together

9
00:00:46.351 --> 00:00:48.451
through a landscape they know by heart.

10
00:00:48.871 --> 00:00:51.431
They are bound, sometimes by blood,

11
00:00:51.471 --> 00:00:56.051
always by a lifetime of shared meals,
shared danger, shared everything.

12
00:00:56.791 --> 00:01:01.511
There are no written rules, no scriptures,
no judges, no police.

13
00:01:01.891 --> 00:01:05.551
Yet there is unmistakably a moral order:
food is shared,

14
00:01:05.591 --> 00:01:08.771
boasting is slapped down, the bully is
brought to heel,

15
00:01:08.791 --> 00:01:11.171
and everyone knows, without being told,

16
00:01:11.171 --> 00:01:14.331
what counts as decent and what counts as
disgraceful.

17
00:01:14.951 --> 00:01:18.911
It works, and it has worked for longer
than we can comfortably imagine.

18
00:01:21.051 --> 00:01:25.551
In the second world, tens of thousands of
people are packed into a single city.

19
00:01:25.951 --> 00:01:27.411
Most of them will never meet.

20
00:01:27.771 --> 00:01:31.931
The woman who sold you a loaf of bread this
morning you may never lay eyes on again;

21
00:01:31.931 --> 00:01:33.951
the man three streets over is,

22
00:01:33.951 --> 00:01:37.251
for all practical purposes, a stranger to
you forever.

23
00:01:37.651 --> 00:01:40.591
It is here that the easy morality of the
small band,

24
00:01:40.591 --> 00:01:44.271
the kind that runs on everyone knowing
everyone, simply cannot reach.

25
00:01:45.031 --> 00:01:46.491
And yet, for the most part,

26
00:01:46.491 --> 00:01:49.951
the city does not dissolve into a war of
all against all.

27
00:01:51.031 --> 00:01:52.551
Something holds it together.

28
00:01:54.591 --> 00:01:58.691
That something is, in large part, a
"dharma", by whatever local name.

29
00:01:59.311 --> 00:02:01.511
Every human society grows one;

30
00:02:01.511 --> 00:02:04.871
but at this scale it takes a particular
and demanding form,

31
00:02:04.891 --> 00:02:06.651
an explicit, teachable,

32
00:02:06.771 --> 00:02:10.411
portable account of how to live alongside
people you do not know.

33
00:02:13.851 --> 00:02:15.991
The puzzle of the simultaneous sages

34
00:02:18.279 --> 00:02:20.431
The great reflective ethical systems,

35
00:02:20.551 --> 00:02:23.311
the ones whose descendants we still live
inside,

36
00:02:23.311 --> 00:02:26.651
did not dribble into existence evenly
across history.

37
00:02:26.931 --> 00:02:30.451
A remarkable number of them erupted in a
single narrow window,

38
00:02:30.631 --> 00:02:33.531
roughly between 800 and 200 BCE,

39
00:02:33.531 --> 00:02:37.191
in civilisations that had little or no
contact with one another.

40
00:02:37.511 --> 00:02:40.331
In North India, the Buddha sat down under
a tree,

41
00:02:40.391 --> 00:02:44.911
and the wandering philosophers of the
Upanishads asked what lay behind the self.

42
00:02:45.391 --> 00:02:47.211
In what we now call China,

43
00:02:47.231 --> 00:02:51.911
Confucius and Laozi were laying down two
rival visions of "the Way."

44
00:02:52.531 --> 00:02:56.851
In Persia, Zoroaster split the cosmos into
truth and the lie.

45
00:02:57.391 --> 00:02:59.571
In the small kingdoms of Palestine,

46
00:02:59.571 --> 00:03:03.591
the Hebrew prophets thundered that ritual
without justice was worthless.

47
00:03:04.431 --> 00:03:06.211
And in the Hellenic city-states,

48
00:03:06.231 --> 00:03:10.151
the first philosophers began prising the
world apart with reason.

49
00:03:12.222 --> 00:03:15.931
The German philosopher Karl Jaspers gave
this window a name:

50
00:03:16.051 --> 00:03:19.291
the Axial Age, the age on which history
turns.

51
00:03:19.791 --> 00:03:24.071
The label, I should say at once, has taken
a battering from historians since,

52
00:03:24.131 --> 00:03:26.971
and I will come back to give the
objections their due.

53
00:03:27.351 --> 00:03:30.031
But the coincidence he pointed at is
arresting.

54
00:03:30.371 --> 00:03:33.971
Why should so many of humanity's deepest
ethical breakthroughs cluster

55
00:03:34.271 --> 00:03:35.331
into a few centuries,

56
00:03:35.691 --> 00:03:39.571
scattered across a continent, among peoples
who could not have copied one another?

57
00:03:40.031 --> 00:03:42.771
Either it is an accident of staggering
proportions,

58
00:03:42.851 --> 00:03:45.231
or something was happening to the human
condition,

59
00:03:45.291 --> 00:03:49.531
in several places at once, that pressed
people toward the same kind of answer.

60
00:03:51.717 --> 00:03:53.331
The two puzzles are joined.

61
00:03:53.671 --> 00:03:56.891
The crowding of the cities and the
eruption of the sages belong

62
00:03:56.891 --> 00:03:58.871
to the same great transformation,

63
00:03:59.071 --> 00:04:00.591
seen from different angles;

64
00:04:00.891 --> 00:04:03.371
how much of the second the first can claim

65
00:04:03.371 --> 00:04:07.551
to explain is a question the evidence will
complicate before we are done.

66
00:04:07.851 --> 00:04:11.291
But to get there, we have to start where
morality itself starts,

67
00:04:11.391 --> 00:04:13.691
which is a very long way before either.

68
00:04:17.311 --> 00:04:19.430
Morality is older than the dharmas

69
00:04:22.211 --> 00:04:26.591
One thing is easy to get backwards: the
codes did not create morality.

70
00:04:27.211 --> 00:04:29.091
Morality created the codes.

71
00:04:31.046 --> 00:04:35.231
For something like a hundred thousand
years before anyone wrote anything down,

72
00:04:35.271 --> 00:04:37.331
human beings lived, mostly,

73
00:04:37.331 --> 00:04:40.631
in small foraging bands and ran a real,
functioning,

74
00:04:40.631 --> 00:04:43.731
demanding ethical order with no doctrine
at all.

75
00:04:44.131 --> 00:04:49.211
Its spine, wherever anyone has looked, was
the cooperative one we met in Part 1;

76
00:04:49.271 --> 00:04:51.711
the content varied from people to people,

77
00:04:51.711 --> 00:04:55.431
as content always does, but the direction
of the rules did not.

78
00:04:55.811 --> 00:04:57.691
We know a fair amount about how,

79
00:04:57.691 --> 00:05:00.691
because anthropologists have studied the
foraging societies

80
00:05:00.811 --> 00:05:03.971
that survived into modern times (not our
ancestors,

81
00:05:03.971 --> 00:05:08.331
but the nearest window we have), and
because the evolutionary logic is clear.

82
00:05:10.031 --> 00:05:14.291
Christopher Boehm spent a career on the
question, and his answer is bracing.

83
00:05:15.051 --> 00:05:16.511
Human beings, he argues,

84
00:05:16.511 --> 00:05:19.631
have the same instinct for dominance as
our great-ape cousins,

85
00:05:19.791 --> 00:05:22.671
the same itch to climb and to lord it over
others.

86
00:05:23.911 --> 00:05:26.751
What foragers do is gang up against that
instinct.

87
00:05:27.371 --> 00:05:30.031
Boehm calls it a "reverse dominance
hierarchy":

88
00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:32.771
instead of the strong dominating the weak,

89
00:05:32.851 --> 00:05:35.611
the many combine to dominate the would-be
strong.

90
00:05:35.931 --> 00:05:39.311
The upstart who grabs more than his share,
who swaggers,

91
00:05:39.331 --> 00:05:43.451
who tries to give orders, is met with a
graded series of corrections.

92
00:05:43.931 --> 00:05:44.791
First laughter.

93
00:05:46.051 --> 00:05:47.011
Then mockery.

94
00:05:48.071 --> 00:05:52.351
Then pointed criticism, gossip behind his
back, the cold shoulder.

95
00:05:53.311 --> 00:05:57.011
And if he will not be shamed into line, in
the last resort,

96
00:05:57.011 --> 00:05:59.611
the group can decide to be rid of him for
good.

97
00:06:01.871 --> 00:06:04.551
Boehm's own trawl of the ethnographic
record turned

98
00:06:04.551 --> 00:06:07.111
up executions among foragers on three
continents

99
00:06:07.211 --> 00:06:09.051
and among tribesmen on three more:

100
00:06:09.051 --> 00:06:11.811
rare, in his phrase, yet widespread.

101
00:06:12.191 --> 00:06:15.051
That last resort was rare, but it was not
random.

102
00:06:15.531 --> 00:06:20.051
Behind it Boehm found a particular and
harder figure in the ethnographic record:

103
00:06:20.051 --> 00:06:22.651
the intimidator who cannot be shamed at
all,

104
00:06:22.651 --> 00:06:27.251
the recidivist bully or killer off whom
ridicule and gossip simply slide.

105
00:06:27.731 --> 00:06:31.911
Most troublemakers fold at the first cold
shoulder; this one does not,

106
00:06:31.991 --> 00:06:35.851
and it was against him that the band held
its ultimate sanction in reserve.

107
00:06:36.211 --> 00:06:40.231
He will return, because he is the deep
ancestor of a human type:

108
00:06:40.231 --> 00:06:44.311
the one who can mimic every signal of
belonging while feeling none of its pull.

109
00:06:45.953 --> 00:06:47.671
This is morality with teeth,

110
00:06:47.671 --> 00:06:50.651
and it needs no scripture because it needs
no strangers.

111
00:06:51.191 --> 00:06:53.491
Everything it requires is carried in the
heads

112
00:06:53.491 --> 00:06:56.231
and mouths of people who see each other
every day.

113
00:06:56.771 --> 00:06:58.731
Reputation does the policing.

114
00:06:59.131 --> 00:07:03.651
The constant low hum of who-did-what, what
we now dismiss as mere gossip,

115
00:07:03.731 --> 00:07:07.031
was in fact the surveillance system that
kept everyone honest.

116
00:07:07.391 --> 00:07:11.451
You behaved, because everyone was always
-- gently -- watching,

117
00:07:11.831 --> 00:07:15.851
and because the story of your behaviour
would outrun you to the next campfire.

118
00:07:17.839 --> 00:07:20.271
The policing runs deeper than tit-for-tat,

119
00:07:20.271 --> 00:07:23.971
and this is one of the interesting
findings in the science of cooperation.

120
00:07:24.551 --> 00:07:27.471
People will punish a cheat even when it
costs them to do it.

121
00:07:27.911 --> 00:07:31.491
In laboratory games where everyone
contributes to a common pot,

122
00:07:31.551 --> 00:07:33.771
players reliably pay out of their own
pockets

123
00:07:33.771 --> 00:07:36.671
to penalise the free-rider who takes
without giving,

124
00:07:36.851 --> 00:07:38.911
a behaviour the economists Ernst Fehr

125
00:07:38.911 --> 00:07:41.371
and Simon Gächter labelled "altruistic
punishment."

126
00:07:42.091 --> 00:07:46.211
More interesting still, the punisher need
not be the injured party at all:

127
00:07:46.291 --> 00:07:48.451
in a later variation of the experiment,

128
00:07:48.451 --> 00:07:52.671
bystanders who had merely watched someone
treat a third person unfairly paid,

129
00:07:52.671 --> 00:07:54.791
unprompted, to punish them too.

130
00:07:56.431 --> 00:07:58.711
On the face of it this makes no sense,

131
00:07:58.711 --> 00:08:01.431
since the punisher bears a cost and
pockets nothing.

132
00:08:01.991 --> 00:08:05.431
But it makes the group's morality very
nearly self-enforcing:

133
00:08:05.491 --> 00:08:08.271
the cheat risks the anger of the person he
wrongs

134
00:08:08.281 --> 00:08:11.051
and the disapproval of everyone who hears
the tale.

135
00:08:11.551 --> 00:08:13.991
There is a self-interested edge to it,
too.

136
00:08:14.831 --> 00:08:17.331
We punish partly to be seen punishing,

137
00:08:17.351 --> 00:08:21.911
because coming down hard on a wrongdoer
advertises that we ourselves can be trusted,

138
00:08:22.151 --> 00:08:25.651
which is exactly why we reserve a special
contempt for the hypocrite,

139
00:08:25.651 --> 00:08:27.511
who preaches the rule and breaks it.

140
00:08:27.851 --> 00:08:32.011
The instinct that polices the band is half
justice and half performance,

141
00:08:32.011 --> 00:08:34.171
and it works all the better for being
both.

142
00:08:34.631 --> 00:08:36.191
Though a caution from the lab:

143
00:08:36.191 --> 00:08:40.591
when helping and punishing compete as
advertisements, audiences prefer the helper.

144
00:08:42.263 --> 00:08:46.191
How did such a society pass on its rules,
with no book to write them in?

145
00:08:46.731 --> 00:08:48.491
Largely by telling stories.

146
00:08:49.691 --> 00:08:52.691
When researchers studied storytelling
among the Agta,

147
00:08:52.711 --> 00:08:54.771
a foraging people of the Philippines,

148
00:08:54.771 --> 00:08:58.331
they found that a huge proportion of the
tales were not idle entertainment

149
00:08:58.611 --> 00:09:01.091
but encoded instructions for living
together:

150
00:09:01.271 --> 00:09:04.431
tales of the sun and the moon learning to
share the sky,

151
00:09:04.751 --> 00:09:06.611
tales of the greedy creature who steals

152
00:09:06.611 --> 00:09:08.911
from the common catch and is punished for
it.

153
00:09:09.691 --> 00:09:13.471
A good story does something a rule cannot:
it tells you the norm,

154
00:09:13.471 --> 00:09:16.711
and at the same time it tells you that
everyone else has heard the norm too,

155
00:09:17.071 --> 00:09:19.071
so that you all know that you all know.

156
00:09:19.671 --> 00:09:24.251
And those same forager societies frequently
have no moralising high god at all,

157
00:09:24.251 --> 00:09:26.511
no watching deity tallying sins.

158
00:09:27.191 --> 00:09:28.651
They did not need one.

159
00:09:29.131 --> 00:09:31.211
The story did the god's later job.

160
00:09:33.051 --> 00:09:37.811
It is easy, at this point, to slide into a
fantasy of the noble savage,

161
00:09:37.811 --> 00:09:40.891
the gentle egalitarian who shames us
moderns.

162
00:09:41.511 --> 00:09:42.931
That is not the picture.

163
00:09:43.471 --> 00:09:47.051
The forager's equality is enforced,
sometimes with violence,

164
00:09:47.311 --> 00:09:50.031
and the ostracism that keeps the peace can
be cruel.

165
00:09:50.491 --> 00:09:52.591
And the warmth was reserved for the
in-group.

166
00:09:53.231 --> 00:09:56.831
The same band that shared everything
within could raid and kill without.

167
00:09:57.411 --> 00:10:01.951
The moral circle was real, and it was
small, and its edge was hard.

168
00:10:04.869 --> 00:10:06.191
The crisis of strangers

169
00:10:08.695 --> 00:10:11.791
The foragers' morality had a hidden
dependency.

170
00:10:12.451 --> 00:10:14.691
It worked because everyone could watch
everyone.

171
00:10:15.511 --> 00:10:18.131
It was a technology for a world of faces.

172
00:10:19.771 --> 00:10:23.211
There is a rough ceiling on how many
people any one of us can really know,

173
00:10:23.211 --> 00:10:24.451
track, and care about.

174
00:10:25.011 --> 00:10:29.471
Anthropologist Robin Dunbar put the famous
figure at around 150,

175
00:10:29.551 --> 00:10:32.291
and although the exact number is always
debated,

176
00:10:32.371 --> 00:10:36.031
and lately the principle too, the core
observation stands:

177
00:10:36.091 --> 00:10:40.491
our capacity for genuine relationships is
finite and not very large.

178
00:10:41.071 --> 00:10:44.051
Below that ceiling, the old machinery hums
along.

179
00:10:44.551 --> 00:10:48.411
Reputation reaches everyone, because
everyone is within earshot.

180
00:10:48.905 --> 00:10:51.351
Above it, the machinery starts to fail.

181
00:10:51.831 --> 00:10:54.951
As the evolutionary biologist Mark Pagel
points out,

182
00:10:55.151 --> 00:10:59.671
kinship and face-to-face reciprocity can
only ever bind a group so big,

183
00:10:59.691 --> 00:11:03.351
which is why no other animal on Earth
lives in cities of millions.

184
00:11:03.831 --> 00:11:06.211
Your family is never going to be that
large,

185
00:11:06.211 --> 00:11:10.511
and you cannot personally keep accounts
with a stranger you will meet exactly once.

186
00:11:12.358 --> 00:11:14.871
And then, about twelve thousand years ago,

187
00:11:14.871 --> 00:11:19.251
our ancestors began to do the very thing
the old morality was not built for.

188
00:11:19.719 --> 00:11:21.131
They settled and farmed.

189
00:11:21.531 --> 00:11:26.091
They gathered into villages, then towns,
then the first astonishing cities.

190
00:11:26.751 --> 00:11:29.551
Almost overnight, in evolutionary terms,

191
00:11:29.771 --> 00:11:34.431
social life stopped being a web of familiar
faces and became a sea of strangers.

192
00:11:35.151 --> 00:11:36.691
You now had to trade, cooperate,

193
00:11:37.331 --> 00:11:40.091
and keep the peace with people you would
never see again,

194
00:11:40.131 --> 00:11:42.431
people whose reputations you could not
check

195
00:11:42.431 --> 00:11:45.151
and whose retaliation you need not fear.

196
00:11:46.031 --> 00:11:50.411
In that world, the cheat has a winning
strategy the forager's cheat never had:

197
00:11:50.611 --> 00:11:53.511
take what you can, and disappear into the
crowd.

198
00:11:55.191 --> 00:11:59.591
The thing we casually call "the problem of
civilisation" is, underneath,

199
00:11:59.591 --> 00:12:01.931
a problem of cooperation among strangers,

200
00:12:01.971 --> 00:12:04.451
and the small-band toolkit cannot solve
it.

201
00:12:05.011 --> 00:12:07.991
Gossip cannot reach a stranger you will
never see again.

202
00:12:08.591 --> 00:12:12.191
Shame has no grip on someone who does not
care what you think.

203
00:12:12.451 --> 00:12:14.771
So the city faced a stark choice:

204
00:12:14.991 --> 00:12:19.331
find a new way to make strangers
trustworthy, or struggle to hold together.

205
00:12:21.031 --> 00:12:25.051
What it found was a dharma, or rather the
kind of thing a dharma is.

206
00:12:25.491 --> 00:12:26.971
To hold a city together,

207
00:12:26.971 --> 00:12:30.591
you need a scale-free substitute for the
village's web of faces,

208
00:12:30.811 --> 00:12:33.631
and an explicit shared code is exactly
that.

209
00:12:33.871 --> 00:12:35.771
It does two jobs at once.

210
00:12:36.271 --> 00:12:41.371
It turns a stranger into "one of us": a
fellow believer, a fellow citizen,

211
00:12:41.591 --> 00:12:44.991
a brother in the faith you have never met
but can already trust,

212
00:12:45.071 --> 00:12:47.451
because you know he answers to the same
rules.

213
00:12:47.991 --> 00:12:51.191
And it makes conduct predictable without
personal acquaintance,

214
00:12:51.371 --> 00:12:54.851
because the rules are stated and enforced
by community and court,

215
00:12:54.891 --> 00:12:56.991
and very often by a god who is imagined

216
00:12:56.991 --> 00:12:59.911
to see everything you do even when no
human is watching.

217
00:13:00.591 --> 00:13:03.871
The watching deity, absent from the
forager's world,

218
00:13:03.891 --> 00:13:08.211
arrives in many of the large societies to
play the role that gossip once filled,

219
00:13:08.251 --> 00:13:11.491
now scaled up to a city that gossip can no
longer cover.

220
00:13:12.011 --> 00:13:15.691
(Whether the moralising gods came before
the great societies,

221
00:13:15.691 --> 00:13:19.691
after them, or in step with them is a live
scholarly fight,

222
00:13:19.751 --> 00:13:21.851
and I take no side in it here;

223
00:13:22.071 --> 00:13:26.051
what matters for this story is the fit
between the job and the tool.)

224
00:13:27.851 --> 00:13:31.031
Why should a watching god, of all the
possible solutions,

225
00:13:31.031 --> 00:13:32.711
have been such a popular one?

226
00:13:33.165 --> 00:13:36.431
The answer lies in the peculiar wiring of
the human mind.

227
00:13:36.771 --> 00:13:41.631
We are, by inheritance, wildly oversensitive
to the presence of other minds.

228
00:13:42.111 --> 00:13:45.291
A rustle in the grass might be the wind or
might be a leopard,

229
00:13:45.331 --> 00:13:49.991
and the ancestor who assumed "leopard" and
was usually wrong left more descendants

230
00:13:49.991 --> 00:13:52.911
than the one who assumed "wind" and was
occasionally eaten.

231
00:13:53.551 --> 00:13:56.191
So we come equipped with a hair-trigger
for sensing

232
00:13:56.191 --> 00:13:59.571
that something out there has intentions
and is watching us.

233
00:14:00.031 --> 00:14:02.011
A god is something like what you get when

234
00:14:02.011 --> 00:14:04.631
that instinct is turned on the whole world
at once.

235
00:14:06.351 --> 00:14:10.471
The cognitive scientist Pascal Boyer adds
a second ingredient:

236
00:14:10.631 --> 00:14:12.671
the supernatural ideas that survive

237
00:14:12.671 --> 00:14:15.471
and spread are the ones that are almost
ordinary,

238
00:14:15.631 --> 00:14:19.751
breaking our everyday expectations in just
one or two memorable ways.

239
00:14:20.291 --> 00:14:23.711
An invisible person who can read your
private thoughts is strange enough

240
00:14:23.711 --> 00:14:26.011
to seize the attention and simple enough

241
00:14:26.011 --> 00:14:29.171
to pass intact from mouth to mouth down
the centuries.

242
00:14:29.631 --> 00:14:32.051
A being like that, always watching,

243
00:14:32.051 --> 00:14:36.391
is the perfect surveillance camera for a
society grown too large to watch itself,

244
00:14:36.391 --> 00:14:40.603
and the human mind was primed to believe
in it long before any city needed it.

245
00:14:44.671 --> 00:14:48.971
The price of belonging

246
00:14:48.971 --> 00:14:51.871
Why are so many of the great codes so
demanding?

247
00:14:52.251 --> 00:14:55.551
If a dharma exists to make cooperation
easier,

248
00:14:55.691 --> 00:14:59.611
why does it so often pile on costly,
inconvenient requirements:

249
00:14:59.711 --> 00:15:02.651
the dietary laws, the dress codes, the
fasts,

250
00:15:02.791 --> 00:15:05.511
the long hours of prayer, the renounced
pleasures?

251
00:15:06.411 --> 00:15:09.511
It looks like waste, but it is in fact the
engine.

252
00:15:11.262 --> 00:15:13.311
The logic was worked out by researchers

253
00:15:13.471 --> 00:15:15.771
studying why some religious communities
thrive

254
00:15:16.011 --> 00:15:17.831
while others quietly dissolve,

255
00:15:17.931 --> 00:15:21.051
and it turns on the same hard problem we
keep encountering:

256
00:15:21.051 --> 00:15:22.731
how do you keep out the free rider

257
00:15:22.731 --> 00:15:25.531
who wants the warmth of belonging without
paying for it?

258
00:15:25.891 --> 00:15:29.571
The answer is to make belonging expensive
in a way that is hard to fake.

259
00:15:30.031 --> 00:15:32.031
Anyone can say they are committed;

260
00:15:32.091 --> 00:15:35.411
only the genuinely committed will keep the
difficult fast,

261
00:15:35.511 --> 00:15:39.031
wear the conspicuous garment, sit through
the long ritual,

262
00:15:39.031 --> 00:15:41.791
and give up the forbidden pleasure year
after year.

263
00:15:42.331 --> 00:15:43.411
The cost is the point.

264
00:15:44.522 --> 00:15:46.951
It screens out the half-hearted and
signals

265
00:15:46.951 --> 00:15:50.551
to everyone watching that here is a person
who can be relied on.

266
00:15:52.789 --> 00:15:55.031
The evidence is unusually concrete.

267
00:15:55.391 --> 00:15:58.551
When anthropologist Richard Sosis studied
a large

268
00:15:58.551 --> 00:16:01.631
sample of nineteenth-century US American
communes,

269
00:16:01.631 --> 00:16:04.391
the religious ones survived, on average,

270
00:16:04.471 --> 00:16:08.711
several times longer than the secular
ones, and among the religious communities,

271
00:16:08.711 --> 00:16:11.791
the stricter the demands, the longer they
tended to last.

272
00:16:12.471 --> 00:16:15.071
Studies of extreme ritual point the same
way:

273
00:16:15.111 --> 00:16:18.751
at a festival in Mauritius where some
devotees pierce their bodies

274
00:16:18.831 --> 00:16:20.911
and haul heavy frames through the heat,

275
00:16:20.991 --> 00:16:24.591
researchers found that the more painful
the ordeal a person underwent,

276
00:16:24.591 --> 00:16:27.311
the more generously they later gave to the
community,

277
00:16:27.351 --> 00:16:30.611
and the more tightly everyone who
witnessed it was bound together.

278
00:16:31.011 --> 00:16:35.591
Later work adds a caution here too: it may
be the felt benefits of the rite,

279
00:16:35.611 --> 00:16:39.071
more than its raw costs, that keep such
practices alive.

280
00:16:39.551 --> 00:16:42.871
Asking a great deal of its followers
strengthens a dharma,

281
00:16:42.951 --> 00:16:45.251
because the asking is how it sorts the
committed

282
00:16:45.251 --> 00:16:48.411
from the casual and welds the committed to
one another.

283
00:16:50.556 --> 00:16:52.291
But this carries a sting in its tail.

284
00:16:53.591 --> 00:16:57.891
Sosis found that the secular communes could
not earn the same return on strictness.

285
00:16:58.351 --> 00:16:59.911
The reason, he argued,

286
00:16:59.911 --> 00:17:02.071
is that religious demands are anchored to
claims

287
00:17:02.071 --> 00:17:05.351
that can never be tested and so can never
be disproved:

288
00:17:05.351 --> 00:17:07.691
you cannot run an experiment on heaven.

289
00:17:08.411 --> 00:17:10.651
A secular code that asks for sacrifice has

290
00:17:10.651 --> 00:17:13.671
to justify that sacrifice in this-worldly
terms,

291
00:17:13.711 --> 00:17:16.371
which experience can always come back and
challenge.

292
00:17:16.771 --> 00:17:21.491
Which means that a secular dharma, the
very thing we are slowly circling toward,

293
00:17:21.491 --> 00:17:23.671
is giving up one of the most powerful
binding

294
00:17:23.671 --> 00:17:26.371
mechanisms its religious cousins ever
found,

295
00:17:26.411 --> 00:17:28.951
and will have to make up the difference
some other way.

296
00:17:29.151 --> 00:17:33.271
That difficulty is Part 7's to answer, and
I shall return to it there.

297
00:17:36.771 --> 00:17:38.371
The sages, answered

298
00:17:41.531 --> 00:17:45.051
Now we can return to the puzzle of the
simultaneous sages.

299
00:17:47.071 --> 00:17:52.111
Set the great ethical eruptions of 800 to
200 BCE against that backdrop,

300
00:17:52.351 --> 00:17:55.991
and the timing looks less like a miracle
and more like a response.

301
00:17:56.811 --> 00:18:00.671
These centuries were precisely when the
pressure of large, complex,

302
00:18:00.691 --> 00:18:05.091
anonymous society was becoming unbearable
in several parts of the world at once.

303
00:18:05.811 --> 00:18:10.571
The cosmic orders we met in Part 1, ṛta
and ma'at and their cousins,

304
00:18:10.571 --> 00:18:13.971
began to turn inward, away from ritual and
toward ethics.

305
00:18:14.941 --> 00:18:18.131
The older religion had mostly been about
propitiation:

306
00:18:18.431 --> 00:18:20.291
perform the sacrifice correctly,

307
00:18:20.391 --> 00:18:23.771
observe the taboo, and the gods will send
rain and victory.

308
00:18:24.471 --> 00:18:27.251
The new teachings cared about something
else entirely:

309
00:18:27.371 --> 00:18:29.911
live rightly, master your own desires,

310
00:18:30.151 --> 00:18:33.131
purify the heart, and thereby align
yourself with,

311
00:18:33.131 --> 00:18:35.191
or escape from, the order of things.

312
00:18:35.791 --> 00:18:39.171
Religion stopped being mainly a technology
for managing the harvest

313
00:18:39.291 --> 00:18:41.851
and became a technology for managing the
self.

314
00:18:43.611 --> 00:18:46.111
Something else was born in that same
pivot,

315
00:18:46.111 --> 00:18:48.811
easy to miss and enormous in its
consequences.

316
00:18:49.591 --> 00:18:52.151
Once you posit an order higher than the
king,

317
00:18:52.151 --> 00:18:56.451
a truth or a justice or a heaven that
stands above any earthly throne,

318
00:18:56.531 --> 00:18:59.171
you have created the means to judge the
throne.

319
00:18:59.651 --> 00:19:03.391
The sociologist Shmuel Eisenstadt put his
finger on this:

320
00:19:03.531 --> 00:19:08.351
the Axial breakthrough installed a permanent
tension between a transcendent ideal

321
00:19:08.371 --> 00:19:10.331
and the grubby mundane world.

322
00:19:11.051 --> 00:19:15.851
With it came a new kind of person: the
prophet, the philosopher, the renouncer,

323
00:19:15.851 --> 00:19:18.131
one who claims to speak for the higher
order

324
00:19:18.131 --> 00:19:22.151
and can therefore stand outside worldly
power and call it to account.

325
00:19:22.831 --> 00:19:25.271
A Hebrew prophet denouncing a corrupt
king;

326
00:19:25.751 --> 00:19:28.391
a philosopher who will not flatter the
tyrant;

327
00:19:28.451 --> 00:19:31.291
a monk who owns nothing and so cannot be
bought:

328
00:19:31.531 --> 00:19:32.991
these are Axial figures,

329
00:19:33.071 --> 00:19:36.491
and they are dangerous to power in a way
the old temple priest,

330
00:19:36.511 --> 00:19:39.171
paid to keep the gods content, never was.

331
00:19:39.711 --> 00:19:43.231
A dharma that answers to something above
the state can always,

332
00:19:43.311 --> 00:19:45.771
in principle, be turned against the state.

333
00:19:47.651 --> 00:19:50.711
Why then, and why in those particular
places?

334
00:19:51.351 --> 00:19:53.611
No single cause carries the whole weight,

335
00:19:53.691 --> 00:19:56.351
but the contributing pressures rhyme
suggestively.

336
00:19:56.971 --> 00:19:58.091
One was money.

337
00:19:58.751 --> 00:20:02.671
Coinage was invented at almost exactly
this time, independently,

338
00:20:02.691 --> 00:20:07.151
in Lydia, India, and China, and with it
came the impersonal market,

339
00:20:07.231 --> 00:20:09.191
the cash transaction between strangers

340
00:20:09.191 --> 00:20:12.291
who owe each other nothing once the coin
changes hands.

341
00:20:13.051 --> 00:20:16.111
The anthropologist David Graeber tied the
age together

342
00:20:16.111 --> 00:20:18.531
as a grim package of professional armies,

343
00:20:18.751 --> 00:20:20.571
the coins minted to pay them,

344
00:20:20.591 --> 00:20:23.211
and the slavery and chronic warfare that
followed,

345
00:20:23.271 --> 00:20:26.931
and he read the new religions as the
counter-movement, the protest.

346
00:20:27.831 --> 00:20:32.051
Once a whole zone of life had been handed
over to naked material self-interest,

347
00:20:32.291 --> 00:20:35.371
another zone arose to insist that material
things,

348
00:20:35.371 --> 00:20:37.991
and even the self, do not ultimately
matter.

349
00:20:38.671 --> 00:20:41.411
The marketplace and the monastery were
born as twins.

350
00:20:43.171 --> 00:20:46.611
A second pressure, less obviously, was
prosperity.

351
00:20:47.511 --> 00:20:51.071
The evolutionary psychologist Nicolas
Baumard and his colleagues found

352
00:20:51.071 --> 00:20:53.511
that the best predictor of where these
ascetic,

353
00:20:53.511 --> 00:20:56.871
self-disciplining religions took hold was
not military might

354
00:20:56.951 --> 00:20:59.591
or imperial scale but sheer affluence:

355
00:20:59.831 --> 00:21:02.691
the energy and security a society had at
its disposal.

356
00:21:03.611 --> 00:21:06.271
When life rises above bare subsistence,

357
00:21:06.411 --> 00:21:11.091
human motivation tends to shift from
grabbing what you can today toward patience,

358
00:21:11.131 --> 00:21:15.571
restraint, and the long view, which is the
very ethic the new religions preached.

359
00:21:15.991 --> 00:21:18.991
Tellingly, these movements flowered in
smaller,

360
00:21:18.991 --> 00:21:22.951
richer polities rather than in the largest
empires of the day:

361
00:21:22.971 --> 00:21:26.651
the Greek city-states, the kingdoms of the
Ganges plain,

362
00:21:26.651 --> 00:21:28.491
the competing states of China.

363
00:21:30.391 --> 00:21:32.091
A third pressure was literacy.

364
00:21:33.131 --> 00:21:36.251
It created a leisured class of scribes and
teachers,

365
00:21:36.291 --> 00:21:38.991
people with the time and the tools to
think about thinking,

366
00:21:39.131 --> 00:21:43.631
to argue, to systematise, to ask why the
rules were what they were.

367
00:21:44.291 --> 00:21:48.431
And running beneath all of it was the
anonymity of the new urban world,

368
00:21:48.431 --> 00:21:52.591
the crisis of strangers that demanded a
new, portable kind of ethic.

369
00:21:53.371 --> 00:21:57.551
Baumard's result, though, deserves its
full weight against my own story:

370
00:21:57.691 --> 00:22:01.031
if wealth predicts the sages better than
sheer scale does,

371
00:22:01.031 --> 00:22:04.751
then the crisis of strangers was one
strong pressure among several,

372
00:22:04.751 --> 00:22:07.711
not the single explanation I may have
tempted you toward.

373
00:22:09.491 --> 00:22:10.711
Put these together,

374
00:22:10.711 --> 00:22:12.351
and the simultaneity dissolves

375
00:22:12.351 --> 00:22:15.671
into something far less mysterious than
divine coincidence.

376
00:22:16.591 --> 00:22:20.471
When broadly similar conditions arise
independently in different places,

377
00:22:20.731 --> 00:22:23.991
you should expect broadly similar
solutions to arise with them,

378
00:22:24.131 --> 00:22:25.691
no copying required,

379
00:22:25.791 --> 00:22:28.131
the same way eyes evolved many separate
times

380
00:22:28.311 --> 00:22:30.891
because seeing is useful wherever there is
light.

381
00:22:31.391 --> 00:22:33.811
The Axial Age was the same problem,

382
00:22:33.891 --> 00:22:37.191
worked out in parallel by people facing
the same predicament,

383
00:22:37.271 --> 00:22:40.031
and arriving at recognisably the same
answer:

384
00:22:40.191 --> 00:22:42.231
an internalised, reflective,

385
00:22:42.231 --> 00:22:46.651
universalising ethic that could bind a
stranger as readily as a kinsman.

386
00:22:48.631 --> 00:22:53.871
I should enter one caution, because tidy
stories about history deserve suspicion.

387
00:22:54.711 --> 00:22:57.691
"The Axial Age" is itself a contested
idea,

388
00:22:57.691 --> 00:23:00.491
and the objections are more serious than a
footnote.

389
00:23:01.591 --> 00:23:05.151
Critics point out that Jaspers drew its
borders to take in Greece,

390
00:23:05.151 --> 00:23:10.491
Israel, India, and China while quietly
leaving out Egypt and Mesopotamia,

391
00:23:10.711 --> 00:23:11.711
older civilisations

392
00:23:11.711 --> 00:23:16.351
with cosmic-order traditions of their own
(Egypt's ma'at among them).

393
00:23:17.141 --> 00:23:18.451
They point out, too,

394
00:23:18.451 --> 00:23:21.291
that the whole notion of a single
spiritual "breakthrough" can

395
00:23:21.291 --> 00:23:25.331
smuggle in a Christian story of history
marching toward enlightenment.

396
00:23:26.391 --> 00:23:28.911
Nor was the idea Jaspers's to begin with:

397
00:23:28.951 --> 00:23:31.491
the Scottish writer John Stuart-Glennie
had

398
00:23:31.511 --> 00:23:35.011
described the same supposed turn in 1873,

399
00:23:35.171 --> 00:23:38.851
three-quarters of a century earlier, and
called it the moral revolution.

400
00:23:39.691 --> 00:23:42.491
And one of my own five exhibits is shaky.

401
00:23:43.191 --> 00:23:45.991
The dating of Zoroaster is fiercely
disputed,

402
00:23:46.091 --> 00:23:51.111
and a substantial body of scholarship
places him around 1200 to 1000 BCE,

403
00:23:51.231 --> 00:23:54.631
some four centuries before the window in
which I have just stood him.

404
00:23:57.091 --> 00:23:59.651
The hardest objection is the empirical
one.

405
00:24:00.351 --> 00:24:02.231
When a team led by Daniel Mullins

406
00:24:02.231 --> 00:24:05.131
and Daniel Hoyer took the traits that
proponents offer

407
00:24:05.131 --> 00:24:07.991
as evidence of an axial transformation and
looked

408
00:24:07.991 --> 00:24:10.511
for them systematically in the historical
record,

409
00:24:11.011 --> 00:24:15.851
they found those traits appearing across
Afro-Eurasia "hundreds

410
00:24:15.851 --> 00:24:20.051
and in some cases thousands of years prior
to the proposed Axial Age."

411
00:24:20.211 --> 00:24:23.471
If the distinctive features turn up long
before the window,

412
00:24:23.471 --> 00:24:26.231
the window is not doing the work the name
implies.

413
00:24:28.011 --> 00:24:29.791
So hold the phrase loosely.

414
00:24:30.131 --> 00:24:34.191
The convergence is striking and the
problem it responds to is real,

415
00:24:34.191 --> 00:24:37.311
but "the Axial Age" is a pattern we have
drawn on the past,

416
00:24:37.511 --> 00:24:38.971
not a switch that flipped.

417
00:24:39.521 --> 00:24:42.651
The truth is messier and more gradual than
the phrase suggests.

418
00:24:43.551 --> 00:24:47.931
What survives is what this part actually
needs: similar pressures,

419
00:24:47.991 --> 00:24:51.571
arising independently, pushed people
toward similar answers.

420
00:24:52.171 --> 00:24:55.611
That needs no magic window, and survives
the loss of one.

421
00:24:58.611 --> 00:25:01.091
What the codes added, and what they did
not

422
00:25:03.291 --> 00:25:06.171
So, did the Axial sages invent morality?

423
00:25:06.601 --> 00:25:09.491
By now you can hear how badly that
question is put.

424
00:25:09.971 --> 00:25:13.751
Morality was already a hundred thousand
years old when the Buddha sat down.

425
00:25:14.411 --> 00:25:17.431
What the new codes did was not to create
the moral sense

426
00:25:17.431 --> 00:25:19.871
but to re-engineer it for a new scale,

427
00:25:19.871 --> 00:25:22.571
and it pays to be precise about what they
added,

428
00:25:22.631 --> 00:25:25.991
because the list is the hidden
architecture of every dharma since.

429
00:25:27.751 --> 00:25:29.151
They added explicitness:

430
00:25:29.511 --> 00:25:31.971
they took an ethic that had lived silently
in habit

431
00:25:31.971 --> 00:25:34.171
and story and stated it out loud,

432
00:25:34.171 --> 00:25:37.691
as doctrine you could examine, teach, and
argue with.

433
00:25:38.071 --> 00:25:41.531
They added universality: they stretched
the moral circle,

434
00:25:41.651 --> 00:25:45.931
at least in principle, past the band and
the tribe toward the stranger,

435
00:25:45.931 --> 00:25:48.571
the foreigner, and eventually all of
humanity,

436
00:25:48.671 --> 00:25:50.971
prising the warm inside of the small group
open

437
00:25:50.971 --> 00:25:53.471
to take in people the foragers would have
raided.

438
00:25:53.871 --> 00:25:58.751
They added reflection: a tradition that
enacted its values and also theorised them,

439
00:25:58.771 --> 00:26:03.031
asked after their reasons, and could
therefore criticise itself and reform.

440
00:26:03.491 --> 00:26:06.391
And through writing, they added permanence
and reach:

441
00:26:06.591 --> 00:26:10.891
a code on a page could be fixed, copied,
carried across an ocean,

442
00:26:10.911 --> 00:26:13.571
and handed to a generation the teacher
would never meet,

443
00:26:13.691 --> 00:26:16.411
in a way almost no spoken story ever
could.

444
00:26:18.111 --> 00:26:21.491
What is on that list matters as much as
what is missing from it.

445
00:26:21.971 --> 00:26:26.091
Every item is a technology layered on top
of the older cooperative human nature,

446
00:26:26.091 --> 00:26:27.531
not a replacement for it.

447
00:26:28.091 --> 00:26:31.531
And that single observation is the
foundation of the secular case.

448
00:26:32.311 --> 00:26:36.731
If a full moral order ran for something
like a hundred thousand years on story,

449
00:26:36.731 --> 00:26:39.571
ritual, reputation, and the levelling of
upstarts,

450
00:26:39.691 --> 00:26:42.571
with no scripture and frequently no god in
sight,

451
00:26:42.831 --> 00:26:45.611
then scripture and god were never
necessary for ethics.

452
00:26:46.431 --> 00:26:50.771
They were tools that a particular kind of
large society found useful for the job.

453
00:26:52.131 --> 00:26:55.891
Which means the job can, at least in
principle, be done with other tools.

454
00:26:57.061 --> 00:26:59.271
The historical record itself is the first

455
00:26:59.271 --> 00:27:01.691
and best argument that you can have a
serious, binding,

456
00:27:01.951 --> 00:27:04.191
demanding ethic without the supernatural,

457
00:27:04.551 --> 00:27:07.691
because for most of the human story, that
is exactly what we had.

458
00:27:09.611 --> 00:27:12.911
There is a complication that keeps the
picture from being too neat.

459
00:27:13.561 --> 00:27:17.211
I have told this as though crowding came
first and codes came second,

460
00:27:17.411 --> 00:27:19.611
society creating dharmas to cope.

461
00:27:20.071 --> 00:27:22.191
But the arrow can run the other way too.

462
00:27:22.891 --> 00:27:24.611
Anthropologist Joseph Henrich

463
00:27:24.611 --> 00:27:27.851
and his colleagues have traced how the
medieval Western Church,

464
00:27:27.851 --> 00:27:30.631
by banning cousin marriage and reshaping
the family,

465
00:27:30.931 --> 00:27:35.391
actively dissolved the dense webs of
kinship that had organised European life,

466
00:27:35.451 --> 00:27:39.231
and in doing so helped manufacture the
loose, mobile,

467
00:27:39.231 --> 00:27:43.631
individualistic, stranger-trusting society
we now think of as modern.

468
00:27:44.311 --> 00:27:48.431
There the dharma did not merely answer the
new world; it helped build it.

469
00:27:49.051 --> 00:27:51.911
Codes and the societies that carry them
make each other,

470
00:27:51.911 --> 00:27:54.691
in a long feedback loop with no clean
beginning.

471
00:27:57.821 --> 00:27:59.691
The fence that comes with the circle

472
00:28:01.931 --> 00:28:04.331
One shadow falls across all of this.

473
00:28:05.071 --> 00:28:08.771
The very machinery that makes a dharma
such a powerful glue,

474
00:28:08.771 --> 00:28:11.111
the way it turns strangers into brothers,

475
00:28:11.431 --> 00:28:14.371
works by drawing a line around the "us" it
creates.

476
00:28:15.051 --> 00:28:17.491
A dharma includes by excluding.

477
00:28:18.151 --> 00:28:23.111
It is never only a circle of care; it is
also, always, a fence.

478
00:28:23.791 --> 00:28:28.071
We will spend a whole part, much later, on
what happens at that fence.

479
00:28:30.151 --> 00:28:33.991
There is a stronger version of that claim
which I had better not make.

480
00:28:34.871 --> 00:28:38.051
Some evolutionary models propose that the
warmth turned inward

481
00:28:38.071 --> 00:28:41.911
and the suspicion turned outward are two
faces of one adaptation,

482
00:28:41.911 --> 00:28:44.111
bred together by war between groups.

483
00:28:44.891 --> 00:28:50.031
It is an elegant idea and it may yet be
right, but it has taken a serious knock.

484
00:28:50.671 --> 00:28:53.631
When anthropologist Mark Dyble re-ran the
best-known

485
00:28:53.631 --> 00:28:56.791
of those models across a much wider range
of conditions,

486
00:28:56.871 --> 00:28:59.571
he found the result held only when groups
were assumed

487
00:28:59.571 --> 00:29:04.111
to differ genetically far more than real
hunter-gatherer groups actually do.

488
00:29:04.651 --> 00:29:09.371
Set that dial to what we measure in the
field, and the altruism stops evolving.

489
00:29:09.891 --> 00:29:12.471
The fence is real, and we have plenty of
evidence

490
00:29:12.471 --> 00:29:14.731
for it in history and in the laboratory.

491
00:29:15.211 --> 00:29:18.051
What we do not have is a settled account
of it written

492
00:29:18.051 --> 00:29:20.171
into our genes by ancient warfare.

493
00:29:23.271 --> 00:29:24.071
Made things

494
00:29:26.521 --> 00:29:29.751
The birth of dharma, in the end, is not a
revelation.

495
00:29:30.331 --> 00:29:31.591
It is a response.

496
00:29:32.731 --> 00:29:36.311
Strip the story to its bones and you find
human beings,

497
00:29:36.451 --> 00:29:40.031
equipped by evolution with an ancient and
powerful moral sense built

498
00:29:40.451 --> 00:29:42.731
for life among a hundred familiar faces,

499
00:29:43.331 --> 00:29:45.651
walking into a world of thousands of
strangers

500
00:29:45.651 --> 00:29:48.071
that their inherited instincts could not
govern,

501
00:29:48.111 --> 00:29:52.071
and building, under pressure, a new kind
of tool to cope.

502
00:29:53.231 --> 00:29:54.971
The dharmas are those tools.

503
00:29:55.691 --> 00:29:59.951
They are made things, forged at a
particular time, by particular people,

504
00:29:59.951 --> 00:30:04.651
to solve a very real problem: how to hold
together a group too large to hold itself.

505
00:30:06.591 --> 00:30:10.351
We are taught to imagine ethical wisdom
descending from on high,

506
00:30:10.351 --> 00:30:13.231
eternal and complete, for us to receive.

507
00:30:13.591 --> 00:30:15.851
The deeper history shows the opposite:

508
00:30:15.951 --> 00:30:19.891
ethical systems welling up from below,
improvised and revised,

509
00:30:19.931 --> 00:30:22.291
shaped by coins and cities and harvests

510
00:30:22.451 --> 00:30:25.931
and the simple arithmetic of how many
people one mind can know.

511
00:30:26.571 --> 00:30:27.551
They were built.

512
00:30:28.191 --> 00:30:31.011
And anything that was built could have
been built differently,

513
00:30:31.091 --> 00:30:32.531
and can be built again.

514
00:30:34.671 --> 00:30:38.051
But I have been speaking in the grand
abstractions of deep time

515
00:30:38.051 --> 00:30:39.651
and lost civilisations,

516
00:30:39.651 --> 00:30:42.211
of pressures and centuries and whole
continents.

517
00:30:42.891 --> 00:30:44.171
It is one thing to argue,

518
00:30:44.531 --> 00:30:48.191
from the safe distance of two thousand
years, that dharmas are made.

519
00:30:48.771 --> 00:30:50.911
It is another to watch it actually happen:

520
00:30:51.051 --> 00:30:53.971
to find a dharma whose making is close
enough to see,

521
00:30:53.971 --> 00:30:56.111
whose founder has a name and a birth date,

522
00:30:56.111 --> 00:31:00.251
whose pressures we can reconstruct in detail
because they fell within living memory.

523
00:31:00.851 --> 00:31:04.711
As it happens, there is one, in the teak
forests of Java,

524
00:31:04.751 --> 00:31:07.651
built by an illiterate farmer barely a
century ago.

525
00:31:08.231 --> 00:31:11.731
If dharmas are made, the Samin let us
watch the hands at work.

